  "content": "In reality, bell peppers, like most plants, do not have genders in the way that animals do. However, the confusion might arise from the fact that some plants, including certain varieties of peppers, can have separate male and female flowers on the same plant, a condition known as monoecious. This means that while the plant itself is not male or female, it can produce both male and female flowers, each with different roles in the reproductive process. The male flowers produce pollen, while the female flowers, if pollinated, develop into fruits (bell peppers). This characteristic of the plant can sometimes lead to the misconception that the fruit itself has a gender."
}

